            <ArticleTitle>Neck pain as a presenting symptom in malignant hypertension.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>51-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Neck pain, unrelated to trauma, is relatively common and is usually presumed to be musculoskeletal in origin. A patient presented with an unusual and serious cause of neck pain-malignant hypertension. The mechanism of the neck pain may be incipient tonsillar herniation of the cerebellum caused by raised intracranial pressure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
